WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.872
Anika: Payment confirmation needs read-after-write consistency here. Historical reports can tolerate older results.

2
00:00:07.872 --> 00:00:14.040
Lukas: What about customer order status pages? They also follow fresh writes frequently.

3
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:20.353
Anika: You're right, include those pages too. That's our blast radius for stale reads.

4
00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:27.097
Priya: Just to confirm, not every report, right? We shouldn't shift all reads blindly.

5
00:00:27.097 --> 00:00:35.065
Hannah: Agreed. Mark delayed reports clearly instead. Never label old data as current.

6
00:00:35.065 --> 00:00:42.097
Raj: Replication lag is climbing beyond two minutes. Maybe replay can't match incoming changes.

7
00:00:42.097 --> 00:00:48.025
Lukas: I'll keep an eye on primary. Limit sensitive reads if capacity tightens.

8
00:00:48.025 --> 00:00:54.578
Priya: A full failover? Let's park that. We have no writer failure evidence.

9
00:00:54.578 --> 00:01:02.186
Hannah: Also check cached status responses carefully. Routing alone won't clear stale caches.

10
00:01:02.186 --> 00:01:08.810
Raj: Okay, compare the positions next then. That should separate transport from replay.

